= Relationships =

Relationships state how knowledge objects connect. Their value is semantic precision: a graph becomes useful when an edge says more than "these pages are somehow associated."

At the same time, an overly ambitious relationship vocabulary creates false meaning. V2 therefore starts with a small set of durable relations and expands from demonstrated use.

== Governing rules ==

# Prefer a specific relation when the meaning is clear.
# Use [[Property:Related to|Related to]] when the connection matters but a stronger semantic claim is not justified.
# Do not infer causality, transitivity, symmetry, or inverse relations unless the property's documented semantics support it.
# A relation is not evidence merely because it is encoded semantically.
# Relationship vocabulary evolves from real content and query needs.
# Distinguish a stored assertion from a derived display, diagnostic, or proposed persistent assertion.

== Core V2 relations ==

=== Related to ===

A deliberately weak association.

Use when two knowledge objects are materially connected but a stronger relation would overstate what is known.

Do not use as a substitute for every missing property; if the same specific pattern recurs, define the pattern.

=== Implements ===

A concrete realization implements an abstract concept, method, protocol, design, or specification.

Examples:

* thermostat → implements → feedback control;
* software client → implements → protocol;
* deployed workflow → implements → method.

The inverse phrase is '''implemented by'''; V2 does not require storing both directions when queries or deterministic projection can derive the display relationship.

=== Part of ===

A component, phase, subsystem, or member structure belongs within a larger whole.

Part–whole semantics vary across domains. Do not assume all <code>Part of</code> chains are automatically transitive in every context simply because classical mereology can define transitive parthood.

=== Depends on ===

A materially necessary dependency: the source object's operation, interpretation, construction, or validity relies on the target.

Dependency is directional. Avoid using it for mere chronological influence or loose conceptual relevance.

=== Supports ===

A knowledge object provides evidence, argument, data, or other epistemically relevant support for another claim/object.

Support should remain inspectable through [[BITwiki:Evidence]] and [[BITwiki:Provenance]]. A <code>Supports</code> edge does not by itself establish that the support is strong.

=== Contradicts ===

A knowledge object materially conflicts with another claim, prediction, interpretation, or result.

Do not make contradiction transitive. If A contradicts B and B contradicts C, nothing follows automatically about A and C.

=== Refines ===

A later or more specific object improves, narrows, extends, or qualifies an earlier model/concept while retaining significant continuity.

Refinement is not necessarily replacement. A refinement may operate only in a particular scope.

=== Derived from ===

A provenance relationship indicating that an artifact, claim, model, dataset, or representation materially descends from another.

Use when lineage is stronger than mere citation. Document transformations when they affect meaning.

== Stored graph state and compiler projection ==

Semantic MediaWiki remains the owner of durable relationship assertions. Scribunto/Lua may validate and project that state, but it does not become a second relationship database.

<pre>
canonical page / Template
        ↓ asserts
SMW relationship property
        ↓ durable graph state
Lua / query layer
        ↓
normalize · validate · inverse display · adjacency/view model · diagnose
        ↓
MediaWiki / graph / BIThub-facing presentation
</pre>

The compiler-facing relationship dictionary in <code>bitwiki-runtime-schema.json</code> contains deterministic runtime metadata such as inverse '''display''' labels. It does not itself assert that an edge exists.

For example:

<pre>
Bitcoin Core → Implements → Bitcoin protocol    stored edge
Bitcoin protocol ← Implemented by — Bitcoin Core  derived inverse display
</pre>

The second form can be generated from the first without persisting a second edge.

This distinction is an invariant:

<pre>
stored assertion
≠ derived display
≠ derived diagnostic
≠ proposed persistent assertion
</pre>

A rule that proposes a new persistent relationship needs explicit semantics, provenance, review behavior, and a write path outside incidental rendering.

== Self-modeling substrate ==

The BITwiki/BIThub technology slice now exercises relationship semantics directly. Examples include BITwiki depending on MediaWiki, Semantic MediaWiki, Scribunto, and Cargo; BIThub depending on Discourse; and BITwiki being related to BIThub.

These edges are conservative real graph state that future relationship panels, graph views, dependency diagnostics, and BIThub integrations can project. They are not demo-only relationships created merely to exercise Lua.

== Relations not yet made universal V2 primitives ==

V1 documented many additional relations, including:

* Subtopic of / Has subtopic;
* Instance of;
* Causes / Caused by;
* Enables / Enabled by;
* Prevents / Prevented by;
* Precedes / Follows;
* Concurrent with;
* During / Contains;
* Located in;
* Adjacent to;
* Originates / Originated by;
* Contributes to;
* Collaborates with.

Many are valuable. They are not rejected.

They are held to the same test as entity types: repeated use, clear semantics, useful query/reasoning behavior, and appropriate domain/range constraints before becoming universal V2 properties.

== Why V2 is conservative about inference ==

The V1 Relationship Types table sometimes described broad relations such as <code>Causes</code> and <code>Enables</code> as transitive.

That is unsafe as a universal knowledge-graph rule.

If A causes B and B causes C, A's contribution to C may be conditional, mediated, blocked, probabilistic, or irrelevant under changed context. Similar problems arise with generic enablement and containment.

V2 therefore distinguishes:

* '''edge meaning''' — what the direct relation asserts;
* '''inference rule''' — what additional information may legitimately be derived;
* '''projection rule''' — how the same stored relation may be displayed in another context.

Inference rules should be documented separately and only enabled when their semantics have been validated. Projection rules may be simpler—for example inverse labels—but still must not silently become stored facts.

== Domain and range ==

Domain/range constraints can improve validation but should not be invented too early.

Examples of useful tendencies:

{| class="wikitable"
! Relation !! Typical source !! Typical target
|-
| Implements || Implementation / Technology || Concept / Method / Protocol
|-
| Supports || Publication / Dataset / Evidence-bearing object || Claim / Concept / model
|-
| Contradicts || Evidence-bearing object / claim || Claim / prediction / model
|-
| Derived from || Any knowledge artifact || Source / ancestor artifact
|-
| Part of || component / phase / subsystem || larger object
|}

These are modeling guidance, not metaphysical laws. If real content requires broader use, refine the property definition rather than silently violating it.

== Relationship quality ==

For consequential edges, ask:

* Is the relationship explicit in a source or inferred by BITwiki?
* What evidence supports the edge?
* Is direction correct?
* Is the target at the right level of abstraction?
* Is a weaker relation more honest?
* Has the relation become stale after a page/schema revision?
* Is the interface showing a stored edge or only a derived presentation of one?

== Composition ==

Multi-hop paths are powerful:

<pre>
Person → authored → Publication → supports → Concept
Technology → implements → Protocol → depends on → Concept
Project → produces → Dataset → supports → Claim
</pre>

But a query path is not automatically a valid logical deduction. The semantics of each edge determine what can be inferred from the path.

